tribute \"ratio_q3_q1_minimum\": type-level note \"at least 40\" — that's in the note conditions; skip (in description).\n\nAlso characteristic \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": \"U0 and D0 (0 × DN upstream and 0 × DN downstream)\". Use \"x\" or \"×\"? Source \"0 x DN\". I'll write \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\".\n\nEnvironmental class & temperature: config_level. But also could be type_level strings. Config-level is better.\n\nNow assemble. I must be careful JSON validity — no trailing commas.\n\nAlso \"documentation folder\" — test_reports entry with pages omitted.\n\ncertificate: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id.\n\nLet me also consider: scheme A — \"This OIML Certificate is issued under scheme A\". ✓\n\nPage total: \"Page 1 of 6\" → 6.\n\nWrite the JSON now.\n\nAlso components characteristics: could attach environmental class etc.? Already in config_level. Keep components minimal.\n\nOne more check — the type line: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4 *\". type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]? I'll use [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"FET4*\"]. Fine.\n\nComponents roles: \"flow transmitter\" (FEW4XX.R) — hmm, one more sanity check on ABB nomenclature. ABB water meter AquaMaster4: \"FET400\" = electromagnetic flow sensor; \"FEW400\" = remote transmitter (wall mounted). Yes — I recall ABB AquaMaster FET sensors and FEW wall-mount transmitters. Also \".R\" hmm — \"FEW4X1.R\"? Possibly \"R\" = ? Not sure but ok. So roles: flow_transmitter → FEW4XX.R; flow_sensor → FET4*. I'm reasonably confident.\n\nBut hold on — could FEW4XX.R be the complete flowmeter and FET4* sensor-only variant? The footnote: \"the second X can be 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only\" — this describes FET4X* variants: integral version (transmitter integrated), remote version, or sensor only. And FEW4XX.R is the remote/wall transmitter. So the certified type comprises the FET sensor and FEW transmitter. Good: components:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4*\"], characteristics? maybe environmental class O, temp -25/+55}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW4XX.R\"], characteristics {environmental class B, temp +5/+55}}\n\nI'll include sub-characteristics in components entries? Schema: components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I can put the component-specific environmental class and temp range in each component's characteristics. That would then make config_level entries redundant. Which to choose? I think component-level characteristics is the most precise home. I'll put them in components and NOT in config_level (to avoid duplication). But config_level is also schema-legit... I'll use components characteristics for environmental_class and temperature_range_ambient (they're explicitly per-component), and keep config_level for power supply, pressure loss, flows, indicating device. That reduces config_level noise. Good.\n\nComponents:\n1. {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4*\"], characteristics: {environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}}}\n2. {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW4XX.R\"], characteristics: {environmental_class: {value: \"B (installed in buildings)\"}, temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: 5, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}}}\n\nCharacteristics inside components: shape? Use same structured form. OK.\n\nNow type_level final:\n- measuring_principle: {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n- accuracy_class: {\"value\": \"1 and 2\"} — hmm alternatively \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\",\"2\"]. I'll keep accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\". Also recommendation.accuracy_classes has [\"1\",\"2\"].\n- electromagnetic_environment: {\"value\": \"E1\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {\"value\": \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"} — hmm, name weird. Alternative: \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\": no. I'll name it \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with unit MPa only, and include the bar conversion in a second attribute named \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative_unit\"? Too clunky. Actually simplest: single attribute maximum_admissible_pressure {value 1.6, unit \"MPa\"}; the (16 bar) is just conversion. To not lose it, description? I'll include second attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}. It's explicit. OK fine.\n\nHmm, or value could be {value: \"1.6 MPa (16 bar)\"} as string — but rule says numbers, units separated. Two attributes it is.\n\n- orientation: {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {\"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {\"value\": \"Sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"} — value string \"intended to measure reverse flow\".\n- software_version: {\"value\": \"03.00.03\"}\n- software_checksum: {\"value\": \"B26AFE6B\"}\n- checking_facility: {\"value\": \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- indicating_range_max: {\"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n- bore: \"Reduce bore\" — the tables say \"Reduce bore\". Include attribute bore_type: {value: \"Reduced bore\"}. Good to capture.\n\nLet me also decide on including \"number_of_models\"? No.\n\nReady to write. Length will be substantial; let me write carefully.\n\nAlso — footnote about wildcard: attach footnote_markers to type designations? Can't; strings. OK.\n\nFor matrix_tables rows keys, use canonical snake_case: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nTable 4/5 row keys: meter_size, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max.\n\nWrite config_level entries in order: power (voltage, frequency, battery range), pressure loss class, pressure loss value, q1, q2, ratio, indicating min, vsi.\n\nActually also environmental/temperature now in components — fine.\n\nAxis names: \"power_supply_option\", \"meter_size\", \"power_supply_option / meter_size / accuracy_class\", \"power_supply_option / meter_size\".\n\nConditions for pressure loss: \"DN40 and DN50\" → value \"Δp 40\"; \"larger than DN50\" → \"Δp 63\". And maximum_pressure_loss: 0.4 bar / 0.63 bar.\n\nNow produce JSON. Ensure numbers: 787.5, 7875, 31.25, 0.1008, 1.008, 20.16, 10.08, 100.8, 15.625, 0.0625, 0.39375, 0.1575, 0.252, 1.575, 2.52, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1.\n\nAlso note battery DN600 class1: Q1 157.5, Q2 252; class2: 63, 100.8. ✓\n\nFinal JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [...],\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"components\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {type_level, model_level, config_level},\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n\nDouble-check revision_history revision strings: \"Initial\" and \"1\" (string per schema example \"0\" not 0).\n\nLet me write it all out now. I'll be meticulous with the 50 condition entries.\n\nFor q2 battery DN600 class 2: 100.8 ✓.\n\nAlso double check AC DN600 class1 Q1: table says 12,6 → 12.6. Yes (not 12.5). And class 2: 6,3 → 6.3.\n\nBattery DN500 class1: Q1 100, Q2 160, ratio 40. class2: 40, 64, 100. ✓\n\nWrite final answer.",
